WEBVTT

c01
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.505
A team has a slow, painful process.

c02
00:00:06.805 --> 00:00:11.229
A faster technology arrives, and they put it on top of the process as it is.

c03
00:00:11.529 --> 00:00:16.551
The problem doesn't go away. It gets locked in, faster and at a larger scale.

c04
00:00:17.251 --> 00:00:26.476
In 1990, Harvard Business Review published an article called Reengineering Work: Don't Automate, Obliterate. Obliterate means wipe out.

c05
00:00:26.776 --> 00:00:32.395
The principle: if you run new technology on a broken process without taking it apart first,

c06
00:00:32.695 --> 00:00:35.648
you only speed up the inefficiency you already have.

c07
00:00:35.948 --> 00:00:40.543
It was written about ordinary computing. It applies to AI just as well.

c08
00:00:41.243 --> 00:00:47.033
Case one. The early 1980s, the accounts payable department of a US car company.

c09
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:53.230
500 people work there. A Japanese company runs the same department with about 100.

c10
00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:57.315
Five times fewer. The US company wants to close that gap.

c11
00:00:57.615 --> 00:01:00.759
Before you see what they found, make the first call yourself.

c12
00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:09.196
Mapping revealed the real reason. Most of the work went into manually reconciling three separate documents.

c13
00:01:09.496 --> 00:01:13.601
The gap was not closed by faster systems for the existing process.

c14
00:01:13.901 --> 00:01:21.547
Instead, the process itself was rewritten: payment is now triggered directly from the receipt record, with no separate invoice.

c15
00:01:21.847 --> 00:01:25.930
Per reports, the headcount needed fell by about 75 percent.

c16
00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:32.911
Case two. A Swedish fintech, per public reports, 2024 to 2025.

c17
00:01:33.211 --> 00:01:39.129
An AI agent starts handling its customer conversations: about 2.3 million a month.

c18
00:01:39.429 --> 00:01:44.877
Per reports, the drop in service load is on the scale of about 700 service reps.

c19
00:01:45.177 --> 00:01:48.514
Handling time falls from about 11 minutes to under two.

c20
00:01:49.214 --> 00:01:56.817
These figures come from company publications and media reports. They're here for learning, not as proof that people should be replaced.

c21
00:01:57.117 --> 00:02:02.715
Then, by early 2025, the company starts rehiring human service reps.

c22
00:02:03.015 --> 00:02:06.821
On simple requests, the AI performed about as well as people.

c23
00:02:07.121 --> 00:02:12.890
On complex ones, like disputes or personally difficult cases, quality dropped noticeably.

c24
00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:15.865
So what went wrong? Decide before you hear it.

c25
00:02:17.765 --> 00:02:22.297
Management admitted that focusing too much on efficiency hurt service quality.

c26
00:02:22.597 --> 00:02:28.685
The fix was a hybrid model: AI for the routine, a person when empathy and judgment are needed.

c27
00:02:29.385 --> 00:02:34.407
Two cases, decades apart, with completely different technology. The same lesson.

c28
00:02:34.707 --> 00:02:37.596
Process mapping is not a bureaucratic step.

c29
00:02:37.896 --> 00:02:42.875
It's what separates a change that works from a change that has to be fixed after the fact.

c30
00:02:43.175 --> 00:02:50.672
And notice: both stories are told in numbers. Headcount, handling time, quality. Without a number, nobody can check a change.
